.TH DTSRDELETE 1 "2026-11-22" "Amiga UNIX add-on" "CDE"
.SH NAME
dtsrdelete \- delete records from DtSearch databases
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dtsrdelete
.B B
|
.B D
.RB [ \-i\c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-o\c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-t\c
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-n\c
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.RB [ \-d [ v ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dtsrdelete
deletes records from DtSearch databases in two steps, named by its first
argument.
.TP 6
.B B
(browse) reads a list of records to delete, shows each and asks whether
it goes; the records confirmed are written to
.I todscrd.lst
(or the file
.B \-o
names), in the same form.
Browsing only reads the databases and can be run at any time.
The list read is
.I shdscrd.lst
unless
.B \-i
names another.
.TP
.B D
(delete) deletes the records of a list,
.I todscrd.lst
unless
.B \-i
names another, after asking for confirmation.
It changes the databases, so it must run when no one is searching them.
.PP
A list has one record a line: the database name, the record's identifier
and a user name, separated by white space; the rest of the line is a
comment.
A word may be quoted with double quotes to hold blanks.
.TP 12
.BI \-i file
the list to read (the file name follows the option directly)
.TP
.BI \-o file
the list
.B B
writes
.TP
.BI \-t N
stop after about
.I N
seconds; an interrupt also stops it at the next record
.TP
.BI \-n N
delete in batches of
.I N
records (default 2000)
.TP
.B \-y
answer yes to the confirmation of
.B D
.TP
.BR \-d [ v ]
print debugging messages;
.B \-dv
a message for each record
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dtsrclean (1),
.BR dtsrload (1)
